addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['What is major trauma?', 'An injury likely to result in permanent disability or death|Any minor soft-tissue injury|A normal response to exercise|An isolated emotional reaction', 0, 'Major trauma is an injury likely to result in permanent disability or death.'],
  ['What does MOI stand for?', 'Mechanism of injury|Management of illness|Measure of intervention|Movement of information', 0, 'MOI means mechanism of injury.'],
  ['What is the main purpose of the major trauma decision tool?', 'Replace every clinical assessment|Identify patients who may benefit from a specialist trauma centre|Determine a patient\'s blood group|Record rehabilitation exercises', 1, 'The trauma tree helps identify and triage patients who may benefit from specialist trauma-centre care.'],
  ['Which is an example of an immediate HEMS dispatch criterion?', 'A small superficial graze|A routine appointment|A person under a train|A mild headache', 2, 'The deck lists a person under a train as an immediate dispatch criterion.'],
  ['Which factors should be considered when interpreting a road traffic collision?', 'Only the patient\'s age|The weather forecast alone|The colour of the vehicle|Impact angle and restraints used', 3, 'Impact angle, vehicle size, restraints and safety mechanisms all affect the energy transfer.'],
  ['What does kinetic energy depend on?', 'Temperature and blood pressure|Mass and velocity squared|Age and height only|Time and distance only', 1, 'The deck gives kinetic energy as one half mass multiplied by velocity squared.'],
  ['What is triage?', 'A method of taking a full medical history|A type of rehabilitation|A method of measuring temperature|Categorising patients by severity and prioritising care', 3, 'Triage prioritises patients according to the severity of their conditions and available resources.'],
  ['What is the focus of Ten Second Triage?', 'Completing a detailed neurological examination|Planning long-term rehabilitation|Identifying the most severely injured and prioritising life-saving interventions|Taking a full medication history', 2, 'Ten Second Triage is rapid and focused on life-threatening injury and life-saving interventions.'],
  ['Which is a Ten Second Triage life-saving intervention?', 'Control of external haemorrhage|Measuring height|Completing a dietary history|Checking visual acuity', 0, 'The two key interventions are haemorrhage control and airway management.'],
  ['What is the P1 triage category?', 'Immediate|Urgent|Delayed|Discharged', 0, 'P1 is the immediate priority category.'],
  ['Who should urgently reassess a patient marked \'not breathing\' during Ten Second Triage?', 'A member of the public only|A clinical responder|The vehicle driver|The hospital receptionist', 1, 'Not-breathing patients require urgent reassessment by a clinical responder.'],
  ['What is over-triage?', 'Failing to record a patient\'s name|Delaying every patient equally|Assigning a higher priority than the patient requires|Using a trauma tag', 2, 'Over-triage places pressure on limited scene resources.'],
  ['What is under-triage?', 'Sending every patient to a major trauma centre|Using a secondary triage tag|Reporting a hazard|Failing to prioritise a patient who needs urgent care', 3, 'Under-triage can prevent a patient receiving medical attention when it is required.'],
  ['What does the M in METHANE represent?', 'Mechanism of injury|Major incident declared|Medical history|Movement', 1, 'METHANE begins with Major incident declared.'],
  ['What does ATMIST support?', 'Airway suction only|A medication chart|A rehabilitation plan|Trauma handover', 3, 'ATMIST is a structured format used for trauma handover.'],
  ['What does the A in ATMIST represent?', 'Airway|Assessment only|Age|Ambulance', 2, 'ATMIST starts with the patient\'s Age.'],
  ['What does SBAR stand for?', 'Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation|Safety, Breathing, Airway, Response|Scene, Cause, Assessment, Report|Signs, Blood pressure, Airway, Respiration', 0, 'SBAR is Situation, Background, Assessment and Recommendation.'],
  ['Why is structured handover important?', 'Missing information can lead to treatment errors and harm|It replaces reassessment|It avoids documenting treatment|It guarantees a normal outcome', 0, 'The deck links incomplete handover with errors in treatment and possible harm.'],
  ['Which is one role of a major trauma centre?', 'Only routine vaccination|Specialist trauma assessment and access to life-saving treatment|Only transport home|Only psychological first aid', 1, 'MTCs provide specialist, consultant-led assessment, diagnostics, treatment and rehabilitation access.'],
  ['Which patient may warrant a lower threshold for a trauma alert?', 'A patient with no injury|A patient attending a routine check|A patient taking anticoagulants|A patient with normal findings only', 2, 'Anticoagulant use is listed as a special consideration.'],
  ['What does SCENE help assess?', 'Only the patient\'s pulse|Only the destination hospital|Only the weather|Safety, cause, environment, number of patients and extra resources', 3, 'SCENE supports the initial trauma scene assessment.'],
  ['What should happen after a life-saving intervention or Ten Second Triage?', 'Stop documenting|Reassess the patient|Ignore new findings|Automatically discharge the patient', 1, 'The trauma pathway depends on repeated reassessment after interventions.']
]);
addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['What is one purpose of pre-hospital trauma care?', 'To delay transport until every diagnosis is confirmed|To replace major trauma centre treatment|To avoid communicating with receiving teams|To provide initial care and move the patient to the most appropriate place', 3, 'Pre-hospital teams provide initial care and help ensure the patient reaches the most appropriate destination.'],
  ['Which finding is listed as a significant mechanism in an adult?', 'A fall from a low chair with no symptoms|Walking a short distance|A fall over 6 metres or two storeys|A minor paper cut', 2, 'The material identifies an adult fall over 6 metres or two storeys as significant.'],
  ['Which fall is listed as a significant mechanism for a child?', 'A fall over 3 metres or twice the child height|A normal playground walk|A small bruise with no mechanism|A routine school journey', 0, 'For children, the deck lists a fall over 3 metres or twice the child height.'],
  ['What does the T in METHANE represent?', 'Type of incident|Treatment given|Time of arrival|Triage tag', 0, 'The T in METHANE identifies the type of incident.'],
  ['When is secondary triage undertaken?', 'Before scene safety is considered|After life-saving interventions and Ten Second Triage, when the Scene Commander decides to commence it|Only after rehabilitation|Instead of airway management', 1, 'Secondary triage follows life-saving interventions and Ten Second Triage.'],
  ['Who undertakes the Major Incident Triage Tool?', 'Only members of the public|The vehicle mechanic|Clinical responders only|The hospital porter', 2, 'The Major Incident Triage Tool is for clinical responders.'],
  ['Which service may contribute to rehabilitation after severe trauma?', 'Vehicle recovery only|Dispatch administration only|Traffic control only|Occupational therapy', 3, 'The deck names occupational therapists among the professionals who may support rehabilitation.'],
  ['What happens to some energy in a vehicle collision?', 'It is completely destroyed at the moment of impact|It can be dissipated by crumple zones and protective devices|It becomes a diagnosis automatically|It changes into sound only', 1, 'Crumple zones, restraints and airbags can dissipate or manipulate energy before it reaches the patient.']
]);
addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['What is the focus of the Trauma Overview session?', 'Replacing all minor-injury training|Managing routine outpatient reviews|Planning long-term community care only|Supporting trauma management in emergency and urgent care', 3, 'The document is designed to support management of trauma in emergency and urgent care settings.'],
  ['Which action is a key message before starting trauma care?', 'Delay all equipment preparation|Complete every intervention on scene|Prioritise scene safety|Wait for vital signs to become abnormal', 2, 'Scene safety is one of the overview document key messages.'],
  ['What should happen to on-scene time in major trauma?', 'It should be kept to a minimum|It should be extended for detailed paperwork|It should be the same for every patient|It should continue until every injury is treated', 0, 'The overview repeatedly emphasises minimal on-scene times.'],
  ['Which phase includes infection and acute respiratory distress syndrome as late causes of death?', 'Late deaths|Immediate fatal injury|Initial scene assessment|Secondary triage only', 0, 'The trauma-death slide lists infection and acute respiratory distress syndrome as late deaths.'],
  ['What is identified as the major cause of death in trauma?', 'Minor skin injury|Blood loss|Delayed rehabilitation|Loss of appetite', 1, 'The overview identifies blood loss as the major cause of death in trauma.'],
  ['How can young healthy trauma patients compensate for blood loss?', 'By immediately stopping all circulation|By lowering their respiratory rate to zero|Through vasoconstriction and increased heart and respiratory rates|By preventing any injury from occurring', 2, 'The document lists vasoconstriction, increased heart rate and increased respiratory rate as compensatory responses.'],
  ['Why can vital signs be misleading after trauma?', 'Vital signs never change after trauma|All trauma patients are already in cardiac arrest|Blood loss always causes immediate hypotension|Young healthy patients may compensate well until late', 3, 'Compensation can make serious blood loss difficult to recognise early.'],
  ['What is the textbook normal heart-rate range shown in the overview?', '10 to 20 per minute|60 to 80 per minute|100 to 140 per minute|160 to 200 per minute', 1, 'The overview gives 60 to 80 beats per minute as the textbook range.'],
  ['What is the textbook normal breathing-rate range shown in the overview?', '2 to 5 per minute|24 to 40 per minute|50 to 60 per minute|12 to 20 per minute', 3, 'The overview gives 12 to 20 breaths per minute as the textbook range.'],
  ['Which risk group is associated with alcohol and drug involvement in the overview?', 'Only newborn babies|Only patients with minor wounds|Young healthy adults|Only people receiving rehabilitation', 2, 'The overview identifies young healthy adults in relation to alcohol and drug involvement.'],
  ['Why are older patients at increased risk after trauma?', 'They may be less resilient and have multiple medical problems and medications|They always have normal examinations|They cannot sustain any injury|They never take medication', 0, 'The overview highlights reduced resilience and multiple medical problems and medications.'],
  ['Why can children be at risk of serious injury?', 'They may have little concept of danger|They always recognise every hazard|They cannot sustain high forces|They never require assessment', 0, 'The document identifies limited awareness of danger as a risk factor for children.'],
  ['Which injury pattern is highlighted in older patients?', 'Only injury after high-speed collisions|Significant injury after a fall from standing|No injury after a fall downstairs|Only superficial injury after any fall', 1, 'The overview states that significant injuries from standing-height falls are more common in older patients.'],
  ['What is important when an older patient has a possible traumatic injury?', 'Avoiding all assessment|Assuming the fall was harmless|Careful neurological assessment|Removing all movement precautions immediately', 2, 'The document specifically calls for careful neurological assessment.'],
  ['What does the overview say about not applying a spinal collar?', 'It always proves the spine is clear|It removes the need for neurological assessment|It confirms there is no head injury|It is not the same as clearing the spine', 3, 'The slide states that not applying a collar is not clearing the spine.'],
  ['Which factors can contribute to serious injuries being missed?', 'A complete and repeated assessment|Unappreciated forces, patient compensation, time to scene, experience and mechanism|Immediate communication with the paramedic|A clearly documented handover', 1, 'The overview identifies time to scene, experience, mechanism, compensation and unappreciated forces as factors in missed injury.'],
  ['What is the suggested on-scene time for penetrating trauma?', 'Several hours|Until all surgery is completed|The same as a routine wound review|Minutes', 3, 'The overview says penetrating trauma should be managed in minutes because it cannot be fixed on scene or in the ambulance.'],
  ['What is the suggested maximum on-scene time for blunt trauma?', '2 hours|60 minutes in every case|20 minutes|No time limit', 2, 'The overview states no more than 20 minutes for blunt trauma.'],
  ['Why should penetrating trauma have minimal on-scene time?', 'It cannot be fixed on scene or in the ambulance|It is always less serious than blunt trauma|It requires prolonged scene paperwork|It never needs hospital treatment', 0, 'The overview explains that definitive treatment for penetrating trauma cannot be provided on scene or in the ambulance.'],
  ['Which preparation helps avoid delay during patient removal?', 'Plan and clear the exit route|Park the ambulance away from the patient|Leave the trolley packed away|Wait until removal begins to find equipment', 0, 'The preparation list includes planning and clearing the exit route.'],
  ['Which item is included in the overview preparation checklist?', 'Turn the heating off|Have the kit and trolley ready|Block the ambulance exit|Delay briefing family and police', 1, 'The checklist includes kit out, trolley out and the ambulance pointing in the correct direction.'],
  ['What is an important principle of scene management?', 'Give every task to one person|Avoid communicating|Match the skill level to the task|Ignore who is holding the head', 2, 'The overview says to involve everyone and match skill level to task.'],
  ['Why are the first 10 minutes of major trauma important?', 'No assessment should occur during that time|It is the only time reassessment is needed|Transport should always be delayed|Life-threatening injuries must be identified and managed rapidly', 3, 'The overview describes the first 10 minutes as vital for consistent rapid assessment and management.'],
  ['What does the overview say about assessment and management in the first 10 minutes?', 'Complete all assessment before any intervention|They should happen simultaneously|Only manage injuries after hospital arrival|Assess the scene without treating the patient', 1, 'The overview states that assessment and management are simultaneous in the first 10 minutes.'],
  ['What does the D in the first DR CAcBCDE step represent?', 'Disability|Diagnosis|Destination|Danger', 3, 'The first D is Danger.'],
  ['What does the R in DR CAcBCDE represent?', 'Resuscitation only|Rehabilitation|Response|Radiology', 2, 'R represents Response.'],
  ['What does the lowercase c in DR CAcBCDE represent?', 'C-spine consideration|Cardiac history only|Capillary refill only|Clinical discharge', 0, 'The overview lists C-spine consideration after Airway.'],
  ['What does E represent in the primary survey?', 'Expose and evaluate|Evacuate only|Exclude every injury|End the assessment', 0, 'E means expose and evaluate.'],
  ['What information is included in the Trauma Priority Call CATMIST?', 'Only the patient name and destination|Call sign, age, time, mechanism, injuries, vital signs and treatment|Only the mechanism and postcode|Only the treatment completed in hospital', 1, 'The overview expands CATMIST as Call sign, Age, Time, Mechanism, Injuries, vital Signs and Treatment.']
]);
addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['When does the overview say 100 percent oxygen should be given to a seriously injured patient?', 'Only after rehabilitation begins|Before checking whether the airway is open|As soon as the airway is clear|Only after discharge', 2, 'The overview states that seriously injured patients should receive 100 percent oxygen as soon as the airway is clear.']
]);
addQuestions('chest-injuries', [
  ['What is the main early problem associated with major chest injuries?', 'Hyperglycaemia|Skin infection|Hypoxia|Dehydration', 2, 'The deck identifies hypoxia from impaired ventilation or hypovolaemia as the most common problem associated with major chest injuries.'],
  ['Which structure may be injured in chest trauma?', 'The heart|The liver only|The knee joint only|The outer ear only', 0, 'Chest trauma can injure the heart, blood vessels, lungs, airways, soft tissues, bones, spinal cord and nerves.'],
  ['What associated injuries should be considered with rib injuries?', 'Abdominal-organ and neck or spinal injuries|Only a minor skin wound|An isolated ear injury|No further injury once the rib is identified', 0, 'The deck lists possible associated injuries to abdominal organs and reminds clinicians to consider neck or spinal injury.'],
  ['What does MOI mean in the assessment of chest trauma?', 'Measure of oxygen intake|Mechanism of injury|Movement of injury|Management of infection', 1, 'MOI means mechanism of injury and helps indicate the likelihood of significant chest injury.'],
  ['Which factors about the mechanism should be considered?', 'The patient clothing and time of day only|The patient height only|The force, energy, duration and direction|The patient preferred destination only', 2, 'The deck specifically highlights the force and energy delivered, and the duration and direction.'],
  ['Which is a possible sign of chest injury?', 'Symmetrical chest movement with no symptoms|Normal oxygenation in every case|Isolated lower-limb tenderness|Abnormal chest-wall movement', 3, 'Possible findings include abnormal chest-wall movement, hypoxia, dyspnoea, wounds, pain and shock.'],
  ['Why can a rib fracture cause inadequate ventilation?', 'It always blocks the upper airway|Pain can lead to shallow breathing|It stops the heart immediately|It increases lung size', 1, 'Pain from rib fractures can cause the patient to breathe shallowly, leading to inadequate ventilation.'],
  ['What does a fractured sternum suggest?', 'That the injury is definitely minor|That no clinical review is needed|That only the arm is injured|A significant probability of other major injuries', 3, 'The force required to fracture the sternum may have caused other major injuries.'],
  ['What is flail chest?', 'Air in the stomach|Blood in the airway only|A segment of chest wall created by two or more rib and/or sternal fractures in two or more places|A superficial chest bruise', 2, 'Flail chest involves two or more rib and/or sternal fractures in two or more places, creating an unsupported segment.'],
  ['What movement may be seen with a flail segment?', 'Inwards on inspiration and outwards on expiration|Inwards on expiration only|No movement during breathing|Upwards only during coughing', 0, 'The deck describes paradoxical movement: inwards on inspiration and outwards on expiration.'],
  ['What is a pneumothorax?', 'Air in the pleural space|Blood in the stomach|Fluid in the ear|A fracture of the sternum', 0, 'A pneumothorax is air between the parietal and visceral pleural layers.'],
  ['What can happen as a pneumothorax enlarges?', 'The lungs become permanently stronger|The lung can collapse|The heart rate must always slow|The chest wall becomes uninjured', 1, 'Increasing pleural air can place pressure on the lung and cause it to collapse.'],
  ['What makes a tension pneumothorax life threatening?', 'It only causes a superficial bruise|It always resolves without treatment|Rising pressure can impair ventilation and venous return|It affects hearing only', 2, 'Pressure can collapse the lung, shift structures and obstruct blood flow back to the heart, causing obstructive shock.'],
  ['Which combination is consistent with possible tension pneumothorax?', 'Normal breathing and improved colour|Bradycardia with a normal chest examination|Isolated ankle pain|Dyspnoea, tachycardia and hypotension', 3, 'The deck describes increasing dyspnoea, tachycardia and hypotension, with reduced breath sounds or chest movement among other signs.'],
  ['Which additional finding may occur with tension pneumothorax?', 'Equal strong breath sounds in every case|Reduced or absent breath sounds|Improved chest movement|A normal chest examination only', 1, 'Reduced or absent breath sounds and reduced chest-wall movement are possible findings.'],
  ['What is an open pneumothorax?', 'Blood is trapped in the pericardium|A rib is bruised without a wound|The airway is blocked by food|A chest-wall wound allows air to enter the pleural space', 3, 'An open pneumothorax follows penetrating trauma when a wound allows air into the pleural space.'],
  ['What is a haemothorax?', 'Air in the stomach|Fluid in the ear|Blood in the thoracic or pleural cavity|A fracture of the airway', 2, 'A haemothorax is blood within the thoracic or pleural cavity.'],
  ['Why can a haemothorax cause both hypoxia and hypovolaemia?', 'Blood occupies space needed by the lungs and represents blood loss|It increases oxygen delivery and blood volume|It only affects the skin|It always causes hyperventilation without blood loss', 0, 'Blood in the chest can compress the lungs while the bleeding causes loss of circulating volume.'],
  ['What happens in cardiac tamponade?', 'Fluid in the pericardial sac compresses the heart|Air enters the pleural space only|A rib moves normally|The lungs over-expand', 0, 'Excessive fluid, often blood after penetrating injury, compresses the heart and limits its ability to pump.'],
  ['Which condition is more commonly associated with penetrating chest injury?', 'Simple wrist sprain|Cardiac tamponade|Tension headache|Minor skin abrasion', 1, 'The deck states that cardiac tamponade is more common in penetrating injury.'],
  ['What is an important early action during the breathing assessment?', 'Cover the chest so it cannot be inspected|Complete a full past medical history first|Expose the chest|Delay assessment until hospital', 2, 'The primary breathing assessment instructs the clinician to expose the chest.'],
  ['Which areas should not be forgotten during chest inspection?', 'Only the patient shoes|Only the forehead|Only the hands|The back and axilla', 3, 'The deck specifically reminds clinicians to inspect the back of the chest and the axilla.'],
  ['What should be checked by listening during the chest assessment?', 'Visual acuity|Air entry in both lungs|Blood glucose only|Knee stability', 1, 'The deck advises listening once to both lungs to confirm air entry.'],
  ['What should be assessed by palpating the chest?', 'Only the patient temperature|Only the pulse in the foot|Only the pupils|Injuries, deformity, instability, tenderness and movement', 3, 'Palpation includes injury, deformity, instability, tenderness, depth and symmetry of movement.'],
  ['What does the deck advise if a major chest problem cannot be corrected?', 'Keep the patient on scene until every test is complete|Discharge the patient|Undertake a time-critical transfer to a major trauma unit|Delay escalation until symptoms disappear', 2, 'Some major chest injuries fail at breathing and require time-critical transfer if the problem cannot be corrected.'],
  ['When should a secondary survey be completed for a time-critical patient?', 'En route to hospital|Only after discharge|Before any primary survey|Only after rehabilitation', 0, 'The deck states that the secondary survey is done en route when the patient is time critical.'],
  ['What initial oxygen delivery is listed in the deck for major trauma?', '15 litres per minute via a reservoir mask|1 litre per minute via a nasal cannula in every case|No oxygen until arrival|50 litres per minute through an unconnected mask', 0, 'The deck lists 15 litres per minute using a reservoir mask as the initial major-trauma oxygen dose.'],
  ['What saturation range is given as the target once vital signs are normal?', '50-60 percent|94-98 percent|70-75 percent|100-110 percent', 1, 'The slide says to reduce oxygen once vital signs are normal and aim for a target saturation of 94-98 percent.'],
  ['What does the deck advise about nitrous oxide and Entonox when pneumothorax is suspected?', 'Give unlimited nitrous oxide|Use Entonox without checking the chest|Do not give nitrous oxide and use caution with Entonox|Avoid all reassessment', 2, 'The deck lists nitrous oxide as contraindicated in clinically suspected pneumothorax and cautions that Entonox may enlarge it.'],
  ['Which is a key principle after applying a chest seal?', 'Stop monitoring after dressing the wound|Delay escalation until the dressing is removed|Apply another dressing without checking the patient|Keep reassessing breathing, oxygenation and circulation', 3, 'Chest-wound management should follow local policy and be followed by reassessment of breathing, oxygenation and circulation.']
]);
addQuestions('drowning', [
  ['Which statement best defines drowning?', 'A respiratory impairment caused by submersion or immersion in liquid|A temporary fear of water without breathing impairment|A cold-related skin injury|A traumatic injury that occurs only below the surface', 0, 'The deck defines drowning as respiratory impairment caused by submersion or immersion in liquid.'],
  ['According to the deck, what does submersion describe?', 'The airway being occluded by liquid|The whole body being completely below the surface|Exposure to cold air after leaving the water|Being covered by a dry protective layer', 0, 'The deck describes submersion as the airway being occluded by liquid; the whole body does not necessarily need to be below the surface.'],
  ['According to the deck, what does immersion describe?', 'An airway blocked by food|Being covered or surrounded by a liquid medium|A spinal injury caused by a fall|A patient who is completely outside the water', 1, 'The deck describes immersion as being covered in a liquid medium, without requiring the entire body to be underwater.'],
  ['What key physiological consequence can follow drowning?', 'Hyperglycaemia caused by cold water|Isolated limb weakness|Hypoxia caused by impaired breathing|A minor skin infection', 2, 'The deck describes drowning as causing asphyxia followed by hypoxia, making airway and breathing priorities.'],
  ['What may happen after a conscious person initially holds their breath in water?', 'The lungs automatically fill with oxygen|The airway becomes permanently protected|Hypothermia is immediately prevented|The urge to breathe takes over and water may enter the lungs', 3, 'The deck describes breath holding followed by an overwhelming urge to breathe and possible entry of water into the lungs.'],
  ['What is laryngospasm?', 'Fluid collecting around the heart|Involuntary closure of the larynx|A fracture of the larynx|A form of hypothermia', 1, 'The deck defines laryngospasm as involuntary closure of the larynx.'],
  ['What can aspirated water wash away?', 'The pericardium|The sternum|The spinal cord|Surfactant', 3, 'Aspirated water can wash away surfactant, which helps keep the alveoli open.'],
  ['What can loss of surfactant cause?', 'Reduced need for ventilatory support|Immediate normalisation of oxygenation|Increased work of breathing|Reduced pressure requirements in every patient', 2, 'Loss of surfactant increases the work of breathing and higher ventilation pressures may be needed.'],
  ['Which event may have precipitated an immersion incident?', 'A convulsion|A routine meal|Improved coordination|A healed skin wound', 0, 'The deck gives a convulsion and traumatic injuries as examples of events that may precipitate an immersion incident.'],
  ['Which factor is identified as increasing risk during a drowning incident?', 'Alcohol or drug intoxication|Early clinical support|A completed risk assessment|A clear route to safety', 0, 'Alcohol and drugs are listed as exacerbating factors.'],
  ['Which factor is particularly important when considering survival after submersion?', 'The colour of the patient clothing|The duration of hypoxia|The distance to the nearest shop|The time of day only', 1, 'The deck identifies duration of hypoxia as a key element in survival.'],
  ['What can cold water and associated hypothermia do in a drowning incident?', 'They make resuscitation unnecessary|They guarantee survival after prolonged submersion|They may extend the period in which survival is possible, but do not remove the need for urgent treatment|They only affect the patient after hospital discharge', 2, 'The deck highlights water temperature and associated hypothermia as factors that may influence survival, while urgent treatment remains essential.'],
  ['According to the deck, does fresh or salt water change the initial management?', 'Only fresh water requires oxygen|Yes, each requires a completely different first response|Only salt water requires airway assessment|No, the initial management is the same', 3, 'The deck states that the historic fresh-water and salt-water distinction does not change initial management.'],
  ['What is the first priority before attempting a water rescue?', 'Removing the patient\'s wet clothing|Safety, including a dynamic risk assessment|Completing a full medical history|Waiting to see whether the patient deteriorates', 1, 'The deck repeatedly emphasises safety first before and during rescue.'],
  ['Which set contains relevant dynamic-risk-assessment factors?', 'Patient name, hospital number and past medical history only|Weather forecast, road traffic and clothing colour|Blood glucose, pain score and medication list only|Number of patients, age, size, time in water, possible air pockets and water temperature', 3, 'The deck lists patient number, age, size, duration, air pockets and approximate water temperature among the risk factors.'],
  ['Why should rescuers keep a safe distance where possible?', 'To prevent the patient being assessed|To avoid communicating with emergency services|To reduce the risk of creating additional casualties|To delay all clinical care', 2, 'Keeping a safe distance is part of the dynamic risk assessment and protects rescuers and other patients.'],
  ['Which communication structure is recommended at the scene?', 'SBAR|A medication chart|A rehabilitation plan|A dietary assessment', 0, 'The drowning deck includes SBAR as part of scene assessment and communication.'],
  ['How should a casualty with a reduced level of consciousness be removed from the water where possible?', 'In a horizontal position|Standing unsupported|Sitting upright on the edge of the water|Face down without monitoring', 0, 'The deck advises removing casualties with reduced consciousness in a horizontal position.'],
  ['What is the stated rationale for horizontal removal of a casualty with reduced consciousness?', 'It guarantees that no water was aspirated|It helps maintain blood pressure and concentrate warm blood in the core|It removes the need for airway assessment|It prevents every form of hypothermia', 1, 'The deck links horizontal removal with hydrostatic pressure, maintaining blood pressure and concentrating warm blood in the core.'],
  ['Which structured approach does the deck use after rescue?', 'A pain score alone|A dietary assessment|DR ABCDE|A musculoskeletal examination alone', 2, 'The deck presents a DR ABCDE approach for assessment after rescue.'],
  ['What injury should be considered after a rescue involving a fall, jump or collision?', 'An isolated skin injury only|No injury because the patient was rescued|A lower-limb injury only|Cervical-spine injury', 3, 'The deck advises considering cervical-spine injury when the rescue mechanism suggests it.'],
  ['What should be addressed early in a post-drowning patient?', 'Long-term rehabilitation planning|Hypoxaemia|Thirst only|A minor bruise only', 1, 'The deck specifically instructs clinicians to address hypoxaemia and monitor airway and breathing closely.'],
  ['Which intervention may be considered when a post-drowning patient needs ventilatory support?', 'A plaster cast|A hearing test|A cold-water immersion|Bag-valve-mask ventilation', 3, 'The deck states to consider BVM ventilation when indicated.'],
  ['Why might pulse oximetry fail to provide a reliable reading after drowning?', 'It measures blood pressure instead of oxygenation|It always reads too high after water exposure|Cold exposure or poor peripheral perfusion may affect the reading|It is unreliable only in warm rooms', 2, 'The deck warns that SpO2 might not read; cold exposure and poor perfusion are relevant clinical reasons.'],
  ['What should be prepared for during the airway assessment?', 'Suction|A routine eye examination|A meal|A lower-limb plaster', 0, 'The deck advises preparing to suction after drowning.'],
  ['What is appropriate for a spontaneously breathing patient after drowning?', 'Close monitoring, treatment of hypoxaemia and hypothermia, and transfer for care|Immediate discharge if the patient can speak|No respiratory-rate assessment if the patient is conscious|Observation only if the water was fresh', 0, 'Spontaneously breathing patients still require close monitoring, treatment of hypoxaemia and hypothermia, and transfer for care.'],
  ['What should be done with wet clothing after rescue?', 'Leave it in place to cool the patient|Remove it to help address hypothermia|Use it to cover the airway|Remove it only after discharge', 1, 'The deck advises removing wet clothing as part of addressing hypothermia.'],
  ['Which temperature measurement may be unreliable after cold-water exposure?', 'The patient age|The number of rescuers|A tympanic reading|The incident time', 2, 'The deck states that tympanic temperature readings may be unreliable.'],
  ['What does the deck specifically advise when a drowning child is not breathing normally?', 'Wait for spontaneous recovery|Only measure temperature|Remove the child from clinical care|Commence resuscitation unless a senior clinician states otherwise', 3, 'The lesson specifically states to commence resuscitation on children unless a senior clinician states otherwise.'],
  ['Why should a drowning patient be transferred for medical assessment even if initially appearing well?', 'Transfer is needed only for paperwork|Complications may develop hours after the incident|A normal initial appearance rules out delayed problems|Transfer immediately prevents all hypothermia', 1, 'The deck warns that complications may develop hours after the incident and advises transfer to care.']
]);
addQuestions('suspension-blast-ballistics', [
  ['Which casualty may be experiencing suspension trauma?', 'A casualty with a minor harness mark and no symptoms|A casualty with an isolated lower-limb injury|A casualty with pre-syncopal symptoms or unconsciousness while suspended in a harness|A casualty with an uncomplicated superficial burn', 2, 'The deck describes suspension trauma in a casualty who develops pre-syncopal symptoms or becomes unconscious while suspended in a harness.'],
  ['Why can immobility in a harness become dangerous?', 'The lower-limb muscles stop assisting venous return|The patient develops an airway obstruction in every case|The patient always becomes hypothermic immediately|The harness prevents all blood from reaching the feet', 0, 'Lower-extremity venous circulation depends on muscle contraction, so immobility can lead to venous pooling and a worsening suspension cascade.'],
  ['According to the deck, when may venous pooling begin during the suspension cascade?', 'After several hours only|Only after the casualty is rescued|Immediately after the harness is fitted in every case|At approximately 3-6 minutes if the legs are dangling and not moving', 3, 'The deck gives an approximate 3-6 minute timeframe for venous pooling to begin when the legs are dangling and not moving.'],
  ['What can worsen obstruction of venous return during suspension?', 'Regular movement of the legs|Harness pressure on the femoral veins and abdominal pressure on the inferior vena cava|A warm drink given before rescue|A correctly fitted harness without abdominal pressure', 1, 'The deck describes increasing harness and abdominal pressure compressing venous structures, including the femoral veins and inferior vena cava.'],
  ['What is the priority for a suspended casualty?', 'Continue observation without planning rescue|Rescue the casualty as soon as it is safely possible|Release the casualty without assessing rescue hazards|Wait for loss of consciousness before calling for help', 1, 'The supplied guidance states that suspended casualties should be rescued as soon as safely possible.'],
  ['What may prolong a conscious casualty\'s tolerance of suspension if immediate release is not possible?', 'Removing monitoring to reduce distraction|Keeping the legs completely still|Giving food before attempting rescue|Elevation of the legs where safely possible', 3, 'The deck states that elevation of the legs by the casualty or rescuer may prolong tolerance where safely possible.'],
  ['Which management approach should be used for suspension trauma?', 'Standard ABC management and early escalation|A full rehabilitation assessment before rescue|Observation without monitoring|A plan based only on the suspension height', 0, 'The deck describes standard UK first-aid ABC management and rescue as soon as safely possible.'],
  ['What is essential before approaching a suspended casualty?', 'A complete medication history|A decision to remove all equipment|A dynamic risk assessment|A decision to delay all support', 2, 'The height, access, equipment, structural stability and rescue capability must be considered before approach.'],
  ['What may cause death after a short drop in hanging trauma?', 'Fracture-dislocation of the upper cervical vertebrae|A blast wave injury|Penetrating injury from debris|Asphyxia caused by compression of the airway and neck blood vessels', 3, 'The deck describes a short drop as causing death through asphyxia from compression of the airway and neck blood vessels.'],
  ['What injury may result from a long drop in hanging trauma?', 'Only a superficial skin wound|Fracture-dislocation of the upper cervical vertebrae|A ruptured tympanic membrane from a shock wave|A temporary cavity from a projectile', 1, 'The deck describes a long drop as causing fracture-dislocation of the upper cervical vertebrae.'],
  ['How should the height of a hanging-related drop be handled?', 'Assume a fixed height definition applies|Ignore the height if there is no external wound|Record the approximate height and include it in handover|Only record it after hospital discharge', 2, 'The deck states there is no definition of the height required for either drop and that the approximate height should be recorded and handed over.'],
  ['What should happen early when managing a hanging incident?', 'Call for help and undertake a dynamic risk assessment|Wait until the patient becomes unresponsive|Move every item at the scene immediately|Delay identifying signs of life', 0, 'The management slide prioritises dynamic risk assessment, identifying signs of life and early help.'],
  ['What may be required to release a hanging patient safely?', 'Waiting until the patient has been discharged|Removing or cutting the ligature when required for assessment and management|Leaving the ligature in place even when the airway cannot be managed|Removing it only when there are no signs of life', 1, 'The deck includes removing or cutting ligatures as part of safely releasing and managing the patient.'],
  ['What takes priority if airway management is required in a hanging patient?', 'Scene preservation always takes priority over the airway|A full secondary survey|Waiting for imaging before intervening|Airway management takes priority over cervical-spine management', 3, 'The deck states that the airway should take priority over C-spine management if required.'],
  ['Why may airway management be difficult after hanging?', 'The patient will always have a normal airway|A hanging incident only affects the limbs|Airway injury and swelling may be present|The airway is protected by the ligature', 2, 'The deck warns that airway injury and swelling may make airway management difficult.'],
  ['What should staff do where CPR is not commenced and the scene is safe?', 'Avoid disturbing the scene where possible to support police investigation|Remove all potential evidence immediately|Leave the patient without assessment|Delay calling for clinical support', 0, 'The deck advises preserving the scene where possible if CPR is not commenced, while patient care remains the priority.'],
  ['What may be required after return of spontaneous circulation following hanging?', 'Immediate discharge|No further airway monitoring|Only a routine limb examination|Specialist on-scene care because the patient may be hypoxic and agitated', 3, 'The deck warns that post-ROSC patients may be hypoxic and agitated and may need specialist care.'],
  ['Which injuries should be considered after hanging?', 'An isolated ankle injury only|Spinal or spinal-cord injury|No spinal injury if circulation returns|A lower-leg fracture only', 1, 'The deck specifically states that spine or spinal-cord injury should be considered.'],
  ['Which is a possible setting for a blast injury?', 'A gas leak or industrial site|Only a sports field|Only a routine clinic|Only a low-energy household activity', 0, 'The deck lists warfare, terrorism, gas leaks, factories, commercial sites, fuel refineries, transport and residential settings.'],
  ['What causes a primary blast injury?', 'Flying fragments only|The patient being thrown against an object only|The blast shock wave|A later infection only', 2, 'Primary blast injury is caused by the blast shock wave.'],
  ['Which injury is associated with primary blast injury?', 'A superficial wound from a knife|A fracture caused only by a fall from standing|A chemical burn from a cleaning product|Blast lung or ruptured tympanic membrane', 3, 'The deck lists blast lung, ruptured tympanic membrane and bowel perforation as possible primary blast injuries.'],
  ['What causes a secondary blast injury?', 'Flying fragments and debris|The blast wave propelling the patient|Radiation only|The patient remaining still', 0, 'Secondary blast injury is caused by flying fragments or debris and may cause penetrating trauma and lacerations.'],
  ['What causes a tertiary blast injury?', 'A projectile creating a temporary cavity|The blast wave propelling the patient against an object|A chemical additive only|A ruptured tympanic membrane caused by pressure alone', 1, 'Tertiary injury occurs when the blast wave propels the patient against objects, causing blunt trauma and fractures.'],
  ['Which injury may be classified as quaternary blast injury?', 'Only a fracture caused by being thrown|Only a laceration from debris|Burns, asphyxiation or toxic-substance exposure|Only a spinal injury from a long drop', 2, 'Quaternary injuries include other explosion-related injuries and illnesses such as burns, asphyxiation and toxic-substance exposure.'],
  ['What does the deck associate with quinary blast injury?', 'Projectile entrance wounds only|Injury from a short drop|Venous pooling in a harness|Effects of additives such as bacteria or radiation, including infection or radiation injury', 3, 'The deck associates quinary injury with additives such as bacteria and radiation, including chemical burns, radiation and infection.'],
  ['How are stab wounds described in the deck?', 'High-velocity trauma with a temporary cavity in every case|Low-velocity, low-energy penetrating trauma|A type of blast shock wave injury|A non-penetrating injury', 1, 'The deck describes stab wounds as low-energy penetrating trauma.'],
  ['Why can a stab wound be serious despite a small external injury?', 'It may cause significant internal damage|A small wound always indicates a superficial injury|It cannot damage internal structures|It only affects the skin', 0, 'The deck warns that stab wounds may leave a small external injury despite significant internal damage.'],
  ['What can a high-velocity projectile create inside the body?', 'Only a superficial skin mark|Only a cervical fracture|A permanent cavity and a temporary cavity|Only a ruptured tympanic membrane', 2, 'The deck describes a permanent cavity along the projectile path and temporary cavitation caused by transferred energy.'],
  ['What can a ricochet do?', 'Guarantee a straight pathway|Make the projectile pathway erratic and increase tissue damage|Prevent energy transfer to the body|Make the exit wound predictable', 1, 'The deck states that ricochet can make the bullet pathway erratic and increase tissue damage.'],
  ['What should be considered when assessing a ballistic injury scene?', 'Only the size of the entrance wound|Only the patient destination|Only whether an exit wound is visible|Scene safety, weapon type, kinetic energy, the crime scene and structures along the projectile path', 3, 'The deck lists scene safety, weapon type, possible anatomical structures along the path, crime scene, patient care and kinetic energy.']
]);
addQuestions('abdominal-conditions', [
  ['Why does abdominal pain require careful assessment?', 'It always identifies one specific diagnosis|It only affects the digestive system|It can range from minor illness to serious or life-threatening disease|It never requires hospital assessment', 2, 'The deck stresses that abdominal pain is common, but the cause may be difficult to identify and can range from minor to life-threatening.'],
  ['Why may an older patient with abdominal pain be at particular risk?', 'Older patients may present atypically and have higher mortality|Older patients cannot develop serious abdominal disease|Pain is always absent in older patients|Age rules out the need for observations', 0, 'The deck highlights atypical presentations and substantially higher mortality in older patients.'],
  ['Why can abdominal pain be referred to another site?', 'The pain must always arise in the skin over the affected organ|Referred pain occurs only after abdominal surgery|Pain can never be referred from an internal organ|Organs may have overlapping innervation', 3, 'The deck explains that localised or referred pain can result from overlapping innervation of organs.'],
  ['What is one purpose of dividing the abdomen into four quadrants?', 'To identify the exact diagnosis without further assessment|To support diagnosis, highlight red flags and aid documentation|To replace the primary survey|To determine the patient blood group', 1, 'Quadrants support diagnosis, highlight red flags and provide a consistent way to document findings.'],
  ['Which is one of the nine abdominal regions?', 'Right hypochondrium|Epigastric|Left lumbar|Hypogastric', 1, 'The nine regions include right and left hypochondrium, epigastric, lumbar, umbilical, iliac and hypogastric regions.'],
  ['Which sequence represents the SOCRATES pain assessment?', 'Signs, Oxygen, Circulation, Response, Airway, Temperature, Examination, Safety|Site, Onset, Circulation, Respiration, Airway, Temperature, Exposure, Severity|Symptoms, Observations, Cause, Risk, Assessment, Treatment, Escalation, Safety|Site, Onset, Character, Radiation, Associated signs, Timescale, Exacerbating or alleviating factors, Severity', 3, 'SOCRATES covers Site, Onset, Character, Radiation, Associated signs, Timescale, Exacerbating or alleviating factors and Severity.'],
  ['What does the S in SAMPLE represent?', 'Signs and symptoms|Allergies|Medications|Past medical history', 0, 'SAMPLE begins with Signs and symptoms.'],
  ['Which information belongs under the E in SAMPLE?', 'The patient exact pain score only|The last ECG result only|Events leading to the emergency call and symptom progression|The examination of the ears', 2, 'The E covers events, including what led to the call, symptom progression and self-care.'],
  ['What should happen if the patient is time critical after the primary survey?', 'Complete every secondary-survey question before moving|Wait for all advanced testing on scene|Stop monitoring during transfer|Move to hospital with continuous assessment and treatment en route', 3, 'The deck advises continuous assessment and treatment en route when the patient is time critical.'],
  ['According to the deck, what is described as an acute abdomen?', 'Any abdominal pain lasting more than a year|Abdominal pain of less than seven days with no diagnosis after examination and investigations|Pain that is always caused by appendicitis|A confirmed diagnosis of bowel obstruction', 1, 'The deck describes an acute abdomen as less than seven days duration with no diagnosis after examination and investigations.'],
  ['What is bowel obstruction?', 'Inflammation of the appendix only|Bleeding from the stomach only|Partial or complete obstruction of the small or large intestine|A weakness in the abdominal wall', 2, 'Bowel obstruction is a partial or complete obstruction of the small or large intestine.'],
  ['Which finding may suggest bowel obstruction?', 'Faecal vomiting, abdominal distension and absence of flatus|Improved appetite with regular bowel movements|A normal passage of flatus with no abdominal pain|Mild isolated headache', 0, 'The deck lists faecal vomiting, absolute constipation and absence of flatus among possible features.'],
  ['Which is a possible cause of bowel obstruction?', 'A healed skin abrasion|Adhesions|A normal bowel movement|A simple headache', 1, 'Causes listed include adhesions, hernias, inflammatory bowel disease, tumours and diverticulitis.'],
  ['What is an abdominal aortic aneurysm?', 'Inflammation of the appendix|A blockage of the small bowel only|A tear in the stomach lining|An abnormal widening of the aorta caused by weakening of its wall', 3, 'An AAA occurs when the aortic wall weakens and stretches, increasing the risk of rupture.'],
  ['Which action is appropriate when an AAA is suspected?', 'Palpate repeatedly to confirm a pulsatile mass|Ask the patient to walk around|Avoid palpating the abdomen and escalate urgently|Delay transfer until the pain resolves', 2, 'The deck specifically states not to palpate when AAA is suspected and identifies it as a time-critical emergency.'],
  ['Which pain description may be associated with a ruptured AAA?', 'Tearing or ripping pain|Itching pain only|Pain relieved by every meal|Pain limited to the outer ear', 0, 'The deck lists tearing or ripping pain and possible pain in the chest, lower back, loin or groin.'],
  ['According to the deck, which observations should be recorded when AAA is suspected?', 'Record temperature in one foot only|Measure pain without checking vital signs|Avoid all observations until hospital|Record blood pressure and heart rate in both arms where appropriate', 3, 'The deck advises noting blood pressure and heart rate in both arms.'],
  ['What happens in an aortic dissection?', 'The appendix becomes infected|A tear allows blood to track into the wall and form a false lumen|The bowel becomes completely empty|The lungs fill with surfactant', 1, 'In dissection, blood flows through a new false lumen within the aortic wall as well as the original true lumen.'],
  ['How are gastrointestinal bleeds divided in the deck?', 'Into upper and lower GI bleeding using the duodenum as the reference point|Into arterial and venous bleeding using the appendix|Into internal and external bleeding using the stomach|Into acute and chronic bleeding using the rectum only', 0, 'The deck divides GI bleeding into upper sources above the duodenum and lower sources below it.'],
  ['Which presentation suggests an upper GI bleed?', 'Bright-red blood per rectum only|Clear urine|Haematemesis or melaena|A dry cough without blood', 2, 'Haematemesis or melaena suggests an upper GI source in the deck.'],
  ['Which presentation may suggest a lower GI bleed?', 'Coffee-ground vomit only|Blood-stained sputum only|A normal bowel movement|Bright-red or dark blood with clots passed per rectum', 3, 'The deck associates bright-red or dark blood with clots passed per rectum with a lower GI source.'],
  ['Which medication history is relevant when assessing GI bleeding?', 'Aspirin, NSAIDs, anticoagulants and antiplatelet therapy|Only moisturiser use|Only vitamin C intake|Only topical sunscreen', 0, 'The deck specifically highlights aspirin, NSAIDs, anticoagulants and antiplatelet therapy.'],
  ['Which presentation is commonly associated with appendicitis?', 'Painless swelling of both ankles only|Right lower-quadrant pain with nausea or loss of appetite|A cough with clear sputum only|Pain limited to the left shoulder in every case', 1, 'Appendicitis commonly causes right-lower-abdominal pain and may include nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite.'],
  ['What is a peptic ulcer?', 'A weakness in the abdominal wall|A blockage of the aorta|An erosion of the lining of the stomach or small intestine|An infection of the appendix only', 2, 'The deck defines a peptic ulcer as an erosion of the lining of the stomach or small intestine.'],
  ['What is diverticulosis?', 'The diverticula are always infected|The appendix is inflamed|The bowel is completely obstructed|Diverticula are present without diverticulitis or associated symptoms', 3, 'The deck distinguishes diverticulosis from diverticulitis: diverticulosis means diverticula are present without symptoms.'],
  ['Why is a strangulated hernia an emergency?', 'It always resolves without treatment|It can compromise blood supply and lead to ischaemic bowel|It only affects the skin|It prevents all abdominal pain', 1, 'The deck states that a strangulated hernia can lead to ischaemic bowel and requires urgent hospital treatment.'],
  ['Which symptom may occur with inflammatory bowel disease?', 'Diarrhoea with blood or mucus and abdominal pain|Only improved appetite|A painless fracture|A normal bowel pattern in every case', 0, 'The deck describes diarrhoea, blood or mucus in stool and abdominal pain in ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease.'],
  ['What is gastroenteritis commonly associated with?', 'Aortic rupture only|A fractured cervical vertebra|Diarrhoea and vomiting, usually from bacterial or viral causes|Permanent bowel obstruction in every case', 2, 'The deck describes gastroenteritis as commonly causing diarrhoea and vomiting and usually being bacterial or viral.'],
  ['What is advised during abdominal examination in the deck?', 'Begin with deep palpation over the most painful area|Obtain consent, consider a chaperone, inspect before auscultation and avoid palpation unless performed very lightly by a senior clinician where appropriate|Skip inspection if the patient is anxious|Palpate forcefully to locate every mass', 1, 'The deck advises consent and a chaperone, inspection, right-lower-quadrant auscultation and only very light senior-clinician palpation where appropriate.'],
  ['Which is an appropriate management and escalation principle for abdominal conditions?', 'Delay analgesia until a final diagnosis is confirmed|Stop monitoring once baseline observations are recorded|Wait until hospital arrival before escalating or pre-alerting|Correct primary-survey problems, monitor trends, provide appropriate analgesia and escalate using SBAR', 3, 'The deck emphasises primary-survey management, full history, analgesia, monitoring, SBAR escalation and pre-alert when needed.']
]);
addQuestions('burns', [
  ['What is a burn?', 'An injury caused only by blunt trauma|A disease affecting the digestive system|An injury caused by exposure to energy|A wound caused only by cold weather', 2, 'The deck defines a burn as an injury caused by exposure to energy such as thermal, electrical, chemical or radiation energy.'],
  ['What is a scald?', 'A burn caused by hot liquid or steam|A burn caused only by an electrical current|A burn caused only by radiation|A burn caused by a blunt impact', 0, 'A scald is a burn caused by contact with hot liquid or steam.'],
  ['Which is a cause of a burn identified in the deck?', 'Only low blood sugar|Only a ligament injury|Only dehydration|Chemical exposure', 3, 'The deck identifies thermal, electrical, chemical and radiation energy as causes of burns.'],
  ['Which type of injury occurs when electricity flows through the body from an entry point to an exit point?', 'A superficial scald|An electrical burn|A pressure ulcer|A radiation-only injury', 1, 'Electrical burns occur when electricity flows through the body from an entry point to an exit point.'],
  ['What may make a chemical burn particularly hazardous?', 'Contact with a non-corrosive warm surface|Contact with corrosive agents such as acids, alkalis or cement|Brief exposure to warm air|Contact with a clean dry dressing', 1, 'Chemical burns can result from corrosive acids, alkalis, bleaches, cement and some organic products.'],
  ['Which feature is associated with a superficial or first-degree burn?', 'Deep tissue destruction with no sensation in every case|A charred appearance through all dermal layers|A permanent cavity|Redness and pain without blistering', 3, 'Superficial burns involve the epidermis, cause redness and pain, and do not blister in the deck description.'],
  ['Which feature is associated with a partial-thickness burn?', 'Blistering, significant pain and a moist appearance|No pain and a waxen appearance in every case|Only intact, uninjured epidermis|A projectile entry wound', 0, 'Partial-thickness burns involve the epidermis and dermis and may blister, hurt and appear moist.'],
  ['Which feature may indicate a full-thickness burn?', 'Red skin that always heals without scarring|A moist surface with normal sensation only|May appear white, waxen or charred and non-blanching|A small bruise without skin change', 2, 'Full-thickness burns may appear white, waxen or charred, are non-blanching and can be painless because nerves are destroyed.'],
  ['Why may burn colour be an unreliable guide to depth?', 'Colour always identifies the exact depth|Burn depth never changes over time|Only the patient age affects colour|Skin pigmentation can make redness or full-thickness change harder to recognise', 3, 'The deck warns that pigmentation can obscure superficial redness or make full-thickness burns resemble unburnt skin.'],
  ['Which is a burn red flag requiring early escalation?', 'A small painless bruise away from the burn|Airway burns, soot or respiratory distress|A normal respiratory rate with no exposure history|A minor superficial burn on an uninjured area', 1, 'Airway burns, soot, oedema, respiratory distress and hot-gas inhalation are red flags.'],
  ['Which burn location or pattern is a red flag?', 'A small isolated superficial burn with no symptoms|A healed scar|A circumferential burn of the chest, neck or limb|A minor area of redness only', 2, 'Circumferential burns of the chest, neck or a limb can compromise breathing or circulation.'],
  ['Which TBSA threshold does the deck highlight as a burn red flag in adults?', 'More than 15 percent TBSA|More than 1 percent TBSA in every case|More than 50 percent TBSA only|More than 85 percent TBSA only', 0, 'The deck highlights burns above 15 percent TBSA in adults.'],
  ['Which TBSA threshold does the deck highlight as a burn red flag in children?', 'More than 1 percent TBSA in every case|More than 10 percent TBSA|More than 50 percent TBSA only|More than 90 percent TBSA only', 1, 'The deck highlights burns above 10 percent TBSA in children.'],
  ['What should be considered with a possible inhalation injury?', 'Only a minor ankle injury|Improved voice and normal breathing in every case|A healed surgical scar|Airway oedema, stridor, black sputum, wheeze or hoarseness', 3, 'The deck lists airway burns, soot, inhalation injury, stridor, black sputum, wheezing and hoarseness as concerning findings.'],
  ['Which primary-survey approach is used in the supplied burns deck?', 'Delay the primary survey until the burn percentage is calculated|Assess only the skin and ignore breathing|Complete DR CAcBCDE and correct problems as they are found|Complete a full social history before airway assessment', 2, 'The deck uses DR CAcBCDE and advises addressing problems as they are found.'],
  ['What history is particularly relevant after a burn?', 'Time, mechanism, contact duration, cooling provided and environment|Only the patient favourite food|Only the patient shoe size|Only the patient postcode', 0, 'The secondary-survey history includes time, source, contact duration, treatment or cooling, mechanism and environment.'],
  ['What is one reason to complete a secondary survey in a burn patient?', 'To replace the primary survey|To avoid taking observations|To decide that no transfer is ever needed|To identify associated injuries, co-morbidities and safeguarding concerns', 3, 'The secondary survey looks for other injuries, co-morbidities, vulnerabilities and safeguarding issues.'],
  ['What maximum cooling period is stated in the deck for a burn?', 'Until the patient becomes hypothermic|Up to 20 minutes|For only 10 seconds|Until all pain has permanently stopped', 1, 'The deck advises cooling the burn for up to 20 minutes.'],
  ['What should be used to cool a burn when available?', 'Running water|Ice directly on the wound|Hot oil|A dry towel only', 0, 'The deck advises running water, with saline if water is not available.'],
  ['What temperature should be avoided when cooling a burn?', 'Tepid or lukewarm water|Clean running water|Ice-cold water|Saline when water is unavailable', 2, 'The deck advises against ice-cold water and recommends tepid or lukewarm water.'],
['What does \'cool the burn, not the patient\' mean?', 'Cover the patient in ice|Stop monitoring the patient during cooling|Cool the whole patient until shivering begins|Prevent ongoing burn injury while avoiding hypothermia', 3, 'Cooling should treat the burn without causing hypothermia, particularly in children and patients with extensive burns.'],
  ['After cooling, what is the correct approach to cling film according to the deck?', 'Discard the first length and apply sheets in a patchwork without wrapping circumferentially|Wrap it tightly around the whole limb|Apply it before cooling|Use it on chemical burns instead of irrigation', 0, 'The deck advises discarding the first length, applying patchwork sheets and avoiding circumferential wrapping.'],
  ['What should not be applied to a burn as routine first aid?', 'Running water|Ointments or creams|A safe temporary dressing|Appropriate analgesia', 1, 'The deck advises not putting ointments or creams onto the burn or scald.'],
  ['What should be done with constrictive items such as jewellery?', 'Leave them in place until discharge|Cover them tightly with cling film|Remove them because swelling may make later removal difficult|Use them to estimate TBSA', 2, 'The deck advises removing constrictive items and jewellery before swelling develops.'],
  ['Which method can be used to estimate TBSA for a small or patchy burn?', 'A full-body percentage estimated only from pain score|The total length of the wound in centimetres|A visual estimate that ignores patchy areas|The patient palmar surface including the fingers, approximately 1 percent', 3, 'The patient palmar surface including the fingers is used as an estimate of approximately 1 percent TBSA.'],
  ['Which value is assigned to each lower limb in Wallace Rule of Nines?', '1 percent|18 percent|9 percent|36 percent', 1, 'Wallace Rule of Nines assigns 18 percent to each lower limb.'],
  ['Which value is assigned to the genitals in Wallace Rule of Nines?', '1 percent|9 percent|18 percent|27 percent', 0, 'The deck assigns 1 percent to the genitals.'],
  ['What is important after an electrical injury?', 'Immediate discharge if the skin wound is small|Assessment of the burn only with no cardiac monitoring|A 12-lead ECG and monitoring because arrhythmias may develop|Cooling the patient in ice water', 2, 'The deck states that 12-lead monitoring is essential after electrical injury because arrhythmias may develop.'],
  ['What types of injury can follow a successful Taser deployment?', 'Thermal injury only, with no possible cardiac effects|Electrical injury and penetrating injury if the skin is broken|Respiratory injury only|Blunt injury only', 1, 'The deck describes a successful Taser deployment as electrical injury and possible penetrating injury if the skin is broken.'],
  ['What is the key management principle for a burn patient?', 'Delay analgesia until a final diagnosis is confirmed|Estimate TBSA but omit the primary survey|Cool the entire patient until shivering begins|Complete the primary survey, cool the burn safely, assess TBSA and convey to definitive care', 3, 'The deck summary emphasises primary survey, cooling, TBSA assessment, appropriate dressing, escalation and definitive care.']
]);
addQuestions('extrication-light-rescue', [
  ['What is the main aim of extrication?', 'To move every patient before assessing the scene|To avoid involving other emergency services|To free and remove a patient safely from entrapment or a restricted location|To replace all clinical assessment', 2, 'Extrication is the planned process of freeing and removing a patient while managing clinical and scene risks.'],
  ['How should light rescue be approached?', 'As a last resort within agreed ways of working|As the first response to every difficult location|Without considering specialist support|As an activity anyone can improvise', 0, 'The deck describes light rescue as a last resort and stresses agreed ways of working and safety.'],
  ['What should never be risked during a rescue?', 'The time taken to complete paperwork|The appearance of the equipment|The patient preferred route home|The safety of the rescuer or the patient', 3, 'The deck repeatedly states never to risk your own safety or the patient safety.'],
  ['Who decides the appropriate management and handling before, during and after a rescue attempt?', 'The nearest bystander|The senior clinician|The vehicle driver|The first person to arrive', 1, 'The senior clinician is responsible for deciding the appropriate management and handling.'],
  ['When should rescue be considered?', 'Only when the patient is stable|When the patient must be removed from harm safely or the patient condition requires it|Only when no specialist agency is available|Only after all treatment is complete', 1, 'The deck identifies rescue as necessary when the patient can be removed from harm safely or the condition requires it.'],
  ['What should staff do if HART or the Fire Service is available for a rescue?', 'Attempt the rescue first to save time|Ignore the specialist agency|Enter the hazard without PPE|Use the trained specialist support and do not attempt a have-a-go rescue', 3, 'The deck says not to attempt rescue if HART or Fire Service is available and never to have a go to avoid calling them.'],
  ['What does SCENE help provide?', 'A systematic scene assessment|A definitive hospital diagnosis|A replacement for SBAR|A method of calculating drug doses', 0, 'SCENE promotes a systematic assessment and reduces the chance of overlooking red flags.'],
  ['What does the S in SCENE represent?', 'Severity|Surgery|Safety|Splinting', 2, 'SCENE begins with Safety.'],
  ['What does the C in SCENE represent?', 'Circulation only|Clinical discharge|Cardiac history only|Cause or mechanism', 3, 'The C represents Cause or mechanism.'],
  ['What does the final E in SCENE represent?', 'External bleeding only|Extra resources|End of treatment|Equipment disposal only', 1, 'The final E identifies Extra resources needed.'],
  ['What is the purpose of a dynamic risk assessment?', 'To remove the need for reassessment|To decide that all rescues are safe|To identify who may be harmed, what further action or resources are needed, who should act and how urgent it is|To replace communication with other agencies', 2, 'The dynamic risk assessment asks who may be harmed, what is being done, what more is needed, who should act and how urgent it is.'],
  ['What should happen to a dynamic risk assessment during a rescue?', 'It should be constantly reassessed and re-evaluated|It should be completed once and never changed|It should stop when the patient is reached|It should be completed only by a bystander', 0, 'The deck emphasises constant reassessment and re-evaluation.'],
  ['Which factor may affect the speed of extrication?', 'Only the patient age|Number of patients, crew, resources, equipment and time-critical features|Only the weather forecast|Only the hospital parking arrangements', 1, 'The deck lists patients, crew, extra resources, equipment and time-critical features.'],
  ['What is the purpose of SBAR during a rescue request?', 'To replace a primary survey|To document only the patient name|To provide a long-term rehabilitation plan|To communicate the situation, background, assessment and recommendation clearly', 3, 'SBAR structures the request for help and communicates urgency and required support.'],
  ['Which information belongs in the Situation part of SBAR?', 'The full past medical history only|The final hospital diagnosis|Why the team was called and a brief overview of findings and hazards|The equipment inventory only', 2, 'The Situation explains why the team was called and summarises findings and identified hazards.'],
  ['Which information belongs in the Recommendation part of SBAR?', 'The assistance required and its urgency|Only the patient date of birth|Only the mechanism of injury|Only the number of observations taken', 0, 'The Recommendation states the assistance required and how urgent it is, including partner agencies where needed.'],
  ['Which resources may HART provide during a rescue incident?', 'Only routine transport home|Only fire extinguishers|Only administrative support|Specialist personnel, PPE, vehicles, command and control and clinical interventions', 3, 'The deck identifies HART specialist support, advanced PPE and vehicles, command and control, triage and clinical interventions.'],
  ['According to the deck, which role does Fire and Rescue provide at the scene?', 'Only recording the patient name|Scene safety and fire and rescue support|Only providing analgesia|Only arranging hospital appointments', 1, 'The deck identifies Fire and Rescue Services as responsible for scene safety and providing fire and rescue support.'],
  ['What may the MPS provide during a rescue incident?', 'Public order, scene security, entry to premises and safety support|Only clinical airway management|Only specialist spinal immobilisation|Only transport to hospital', 0, 'The deck lists public order, scene security, entry to premises, safety support and crime-scene continuity.'],
  ['What is USAR trained to deal with?', 'Only routine road traffic collisions|Only minor household injuries|Collapsed or unsafe structures, work at height and confined spaces|Only waterborne illness', 2, 'USAR is trained for collapsed structures, unsafe structures, work at height and confined spaces.'],
  ['Which item is listed as light-rescue equipment?', 'A routine medication chart|A food thermometer|A hearing aid|An orthopaedic stretcher or rescue board', 3, 'The equipment list includes orthopaedic stretchers and rescue boards.'],
  ['Which item is identified as HART equipment in the deck?', 'The Kendrick Extrication Device|A standard blanket|A routine pulse oximeter|A hospital meal tray', 0, 'The deck identifies the Kendrick Extrication Device as HART equipment.'],
  ['What should equipment selection be based on?', 'Only the colour of the equipment|The patient, entrapment, environment, access, egress and available team|Only the patient age|Only the nearest hospital', 1, 'Equipment and planning must reflect the patient, hazard, route, team and rescue requirements.'],
  ['When may self-extrication be appropriate for a patient?', 'Whenever the patient asks, regardless of injury|Only when no assessment has occurred|When the patient can understand instructions, move safely, use a safe route and has no overriding mechanism or injury concern|Only after specialist rescue is refused', 2, 'Self-extrication depends on patient ability, understanding, mechanism, injuries, hazards and a safe route.'],
  ['What should remain continuous during patient movement?', 'The patient social history only|The equipment inventory|The police investigation only|Clinical assessment and management', 3, 'The patient condition remains the team responsibility throughout the rescue, with continuous assessment and management.'],
  ['Which factors should be considered when planning immobilisation and patient movement?', 'Only whether the patient is comfortable|Suspected injury, body support, movement, equipment and the need to remove the patient from harm|Only the distance to the ambulance|Only the weather', 1, 'Immobilisation planning must balance suspected injury, movement precautions, equipment and safe removal from harm.'],
  ['When may crash-helmet removal be considered?', 'When required for airway or head assessment under trained local guidance|To make the patient more comfortable in every case|To avoid calling a specialist agency|To complete paperwork more quickly', 0, 'The deck identifies helmet removal as a learning area; any removal should follow trained local guidance and senior clinician direction.'],
  ['Which potential complication should be considered during crash-helmet removal?', 'Guaranteed improvement in spinal stability|Prevention of all airway problems|Unwanted head and neck movement or worsening airway compromise|Improved circulation in every patient', 2, 'Potential risks include unwanted movement, aggravating spinal injury and worsening airway management.'],
  ['Which combination of findings should trigger early escalation and prioritised rescue planning?', 'A stable patient with no hazards|Airway compromise, shock, reduced consciousness or a dangerous environment|A completed equipment check only|A normal observation with no mechanism', 1, 'Time-critical features include airway or breathing compromise, haemorrhage, shock, reduced consciousness and dangerous environments.'],
  ['What should happen after the patient has been extricated?', 'Stop monitoring because the rescue is complete|Remove all documentation|Assume no injury was caused by the entrapment|Repeat assessment, reassess injuries and hand over the mechanism, findings and interventions', 3, 'After rescue, reassess the patient, check for hidden injury, review observations and provide a structured handover.']
]);
